":"https://adriansieber.com/announcing-perspec-1-0/","score":35,"flags":0,"comment_count":17,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"bdesham","user_is_author":false,"tags":["graphics","haskell","release"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/plmwzl","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/plmwzl/perspec_1_0_haskell_desktop_app_for"},{"short_id":"gxcqxj","created_at":"2026-08-25T04:33:23.657-05:00","title":"Galaxium, a WebGPU universe explorer","url":"https://galaxium.app","score":4,"flags":0,"comment_count":0,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"guillaumec","user_is_author":true,"tags":["wasm"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/gxcqxj","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/gxcqxj/galaxium_webgpu_universe_explorer"},{"short_id":"b15udy","created_at":"2026-08-25T05:46:18.571-05:00","title":"Another look at SQLite's WAL-Reset bug","url":"https://theconsensus.dev/p/2026/08/23/another-look-at-sqlite-wal-reset.html","score":5,"flags":0,"comment_count":1,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"ohrv","user_is_author":false,"tags":["databases"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/b15udy","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/b15udy/another_look_at_sqlite_s_wal_reset_bug"},{"short_id":"oqv9np","created_at":"2026-08-24T10:24:13.663-05:00","title":"IPython is All You Need","url":"https://nathancooper.io/blog/2026-08-10-ipython-is-all-you-need","score":16,"flags":9,"comment_count":6,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"ncoop57","user_is_author":true,"tags":["python","vibecoding"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/oqv9np","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/oqv9np/ipython_is_all_you_need"},{"short_id":"w0d3um","created_at":"2026-08-24T21:44:14.638-05:00","title":"POP-2000, a lingua-franca POP-2 dialect","url":"https://hitogata.neocities.org/POP-2000","score":13,"flags":0,"comment_count":1,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"Internet_Janitor","user_is_author":false,"tags":["concatenative","plt"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/w0d3um","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/w0d3um/pop_2000_lingua_franca_pop_2_dialect"},{"short_id":"al6sdh","created_at":"2026-08-24T16:28:12.877-05:00","title":"distributed identity","url":"https://jyn.dev/distributed-identity/","score":21,"flags":0,"comment_count":2,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"hugoarnal","user_is_author":false,"tags":["distributed","vcs"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/al6sdh","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/al6sdh/distributed_identity"},{"short_id":"daohlz","created_at":"2026-08-24T14:08:59.286-05:00","title":"Routeup – stable local HTTPS URLs and opt-in public tunnels","url":"https://routeup.dev","score":6,"flags":5,"comment_count":6,"description":"\u003cp\u003eI built Routeup around a simple idea: local apps should use their real names.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIt was inspired by \u003ca href=\"https://portless.sh/\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003ePortless\u003c/a\u003e, but I also wanted built-in public tunneling, selective path exposure, request inspection, and live logs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eInstead of opening \u003ccode\u003elocalhost:3000\u003c/code\u003e, I can give a project a stable, trusted HTTPS URL and keep using it across restarts:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre lang=\"text\"\u003e\u003ccode\u003ehttps://example-app.localhost\r\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe app stays local by default. When an external service needs access, I can expose the whole route or only selected paths such as \u003ccode\u003e/api/webhooks/*\u003c/code\u003e:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre lang=\"text\"\u003e\u003ccode\u003elocal:  https://example-app.localhost\r\npublic: https://example-app.try.routeup.dev\r\nexpose: /api/webhooks/*\r\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eExposure has its own lifecycle. I can expose an already-running route from another terminal and stop sharing it without stopping the local app.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFor quick, temporary exposure, Routeup provides names under \u003ccode\u003e*.try.routeup.dev\u003c/code\u003e without requiring an account or token. For stable public names, a token claims a namespace such as \u003ccode\u003eteam.routeup.dev\u003c/code\u003e. Apps can then use names beneath it, such as \u003ccode\u003eapi.team.routeup.dev\u003c/code\u003e and \u003ccode\u003edocs.team.routeup.dev\u003c/code\u003e. The same namespace model works with a self-hosted Routeup server and your own domain.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eRouteup grew out of frustrations I had using ngrok and localtunnel. They could expose a port, but their tunnel-first model was not the workflow I wanted. I wanted the app to have a stable local identity first, with public access added only when and where it was needed.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI use this workflow heavily at work and for personal projects. Routeup currently supports multiple path targets behind one origin, repository-level configuration, running an app command, request inspection, hosted public URLs, and self-hosted servers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe local workflow requires no account, token, DNS, or remote server. Routeup is a single Go binary, has no CLI telemetry, and is MIT licensed.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://routeup.dev\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003eWebsite and documentation\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://github.com/mukul-mehta/routeup\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003eSource code\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI'd appreciate any feedback on the project, especially ideas for improving the workflow, documentation, or features. If you run into any issues during setup or while using the tool, please \u003ca href=\"https://github.com/mukul-mehta/routeup/issues/new/choose\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003eopen a GitHub issue\u003c/a\u003e or email \u003ca href=\"mailto:help@routeup.dev\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003ehelp@routeup.dev\u003c/a\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n","description_plain":"I built Routeup around a simple idea: local apps should use their real names.\r\n\r\nIt was inspired by [Portless](https://portless.sh/), but I also wanted built-in public tunneling, selective path exposure, request inspection, and live logs.\r\n\r\nInstead of opening `localhost:3000`, I can give a project a stable, trusted HTTPS URL and keep using it across restarts:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nhttps://example-app.localhost\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe app stays local by default. When an external service needs access, I can expose the whole route or only selected paths such as `/api/webhooks/*`:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nlocal:  https://example-app.localhost\r\npublic: https://example-app.try.routeup.dev\r\nexpose: /api/webhooks/*\r\n```\r\n\r\nExposure has its own lifecycle. I can expose an already-running route from another terminal and stop sharing it without stopping the local app.\r\n\r\nFor quick, temporary exposure, Routeup provides names under `*.try.routeup.dev` without requiring an account or token. For stable public names, a token claims a namespace such as `team.routeup.dev`. Apps can then use names beneath it, such as `api.team.routeup.dev` and `docs.team.routeup.dev`. The same namespace model works with a self-hosted Routeup server and your own domain.\r\n\r\nRouteup grew out of frustrations I had using ngrok and localtunnel. They could expose a port, but their tunnel-first model was not the workflow I wanted. I wanted the app to have a stable local identity first, with public access added only when and where it was needed.\r\n\r\nI use this workflow heavily at work and for personal projects. Routeup currently supports multiple path targets behind one origin, repository-level configuration, running an app command, request inspection, hosted public URLs, and self-hosted servers.\r\n\r\nThe local workflow requires no account, token, DNS, or remote server. Routeup is a single Go binary, has no CLI telemetry, and is MIT licensed.\r\n\r\n- [Website and documentation](https://routeup.dev)\r\n- [Source code](https://github.com/mukul-mehta/routeup)\r\n\r\nI'd appreciate any feedback on the project, especially ideas for improving the workflow, documentation, or features. If you run into any issues during setup or while using the tool, please [open a GitHub issue](https://github.com/mukul-mehta/routeup/issues/new/choose) or email [help@routeup.dev](mailto:help@routeup.dev).","submitter_user":"mukul","user_is_author":true,"tags":["go","networking","show","vibecoding"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/daohlz","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/daohlz/routeup_stable_local_https_urls_opt"},{"short_id":"swzhtb","created_at":"2026-08-24T09:36:35.686-05:00","title":"Dynamically Naming Servers","url":"https://arch.dog/bark/dynamically-naming-servers","score":13,"flags":0,"comment_count":4,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"gmem","user_is_author":true,"tags":["devops"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/swzhtb","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/swzhtb/dynamically_naming_servers"},{"short_id":"4haxto","created_at":"2026-08-25T03:04:13.837-05:00","title":"A Design Space Exploration of Async/Await","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.20677","score":4,"flags":0,"comment_count":0,"description":"\u003cp\u003eAbstract: Many modern programming languages include some form of asynchronous programming. In particular, a growing number now have what we call straight-line asynchrony: attempts to provide asynchronous functions that look similar to synchronous functions, thereby enabling asynchrony without introducing complex control. These languages often share construct names like “async” and “await,” which suggests that they have deep semantic similarities. Yet, a close examination reveals that these languages are quite different along several dimensions, often subtly. These differences have real semantic consequences: similar-looking programs can exhibit divergent behavior, confusing developers and language designers alike.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis paper therefore presents a design space exploration of straight-line asynchrony. We dissect several existing languages, and show how no two of them agree as a whole on design decisions that affect the presence and ordering of execution. We articulate a design space with nine dimensions covering the full lifecycle of an asynchronous computation, covering questions such as: What precise guarantees does a language give upon calling an asynchronous function? What happens at the end of a task’s life? How can a task handle being cancelled? We explore these questions through concrete examples, informal design discussion, and a formal semantics. Our ultimate goal is to help programmers, language designers, and language theorists all better understand the emerging landscape of straight-line asynchrony.\u003c/p\u003e\n","description_plain":"Abstract: Many modern programming languages include some form of asynchronous programming. In particular, a growing number now have what we call straight-line asynchrony: attempts to provide asynchronous functions that look similar to synchronous functions, thereby enabling asynchrony without introducing complex control. These languages often share construct names like “async” and “await,” which suggests that they have deep semantic similarities. Yet, a close examination reveals that these languages are quite different along several dimensions, often subtly. These differences have real semantic consequences: similar-looking programs can exhibit divergent behavior, confusing developers and language designers alike.\r\n\r\nThis paper therefore presents a design space exploration of straight-line asynchrony. We dissect several existing languages, and show how no two of them agree as a whole on design decisions that affect the presence and ordering of execution. We articulate a design space with nine dimensions covering the full lifecycle of an asynchronous computation, covering questions such as: What precise guarantees does a language give upon calling an asynchronous function? What happens at the end of a task’s life? How can a task handle being cancelled? We explore these questions through concrete examples, informal design discussion, and a formal semantics. Our ultimate goal is to help programmers, language designers, and language theorists all better understand the emerging landscape of straight-line asynchrony.","submitter_user":"asb","user_is_author":false,"tags":["pdf","plt"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/4haxto","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/4haxto/design_space_exploration_async_await"},{"short_id":"vcdk2q","created_at":"2026-08-23T22:22:13.331-05:00","title":"Everything I own, owned","url":"https://schlarp.com/posts/everything-i-own-owned/","score":61,"flags":0,"comment_count":21,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"wonk","user_is_author":false,"tags":["security","vibecoding"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/vcdk2q","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/vcdk2q/everything_i_own_owned"},{"short_id":"uodrjx","created_at":"2026-08-24T15:53:40.676-05:00","title":"kudu - easily manage VMs on Linux","url":"https://github.com/pythops/kudu","score":7,"flags":0,"comment_count":0,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"pythops","user_is_author":true,"tags":["rust","virtualization"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/uodrjx","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/uodrjx/kudu_easily_manage_vms_on_linux"},{"short_id":"fyps8w","created_at":"2026-08-25T01:42:59.564-05:00","title":"Haskell diagrams: Tessellations","url":"https://mgarletmilani.com/blog/2026-06-03-tessellations/","score":3,"flags":0,"comment_count":1,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"abhin4v","user_is_author":false,"tags":["art","haskell"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/fyps8w","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/fyps8w/haskell_diagrams_tessellations"},{"short_id":"nfmlwp","created_at":"2026-08-24T20:06:46.201-05:00","title":"Hot Chips 2026: CUDA t